# Pushanova Website

Edit this file. Keep it short. Prefer facts over style lectures.

## Project

Pushanova is a native push-up training app for iPhone, iPad, and Apple Watch. 
The core value proposition is removing friction: no tripods, no accounts, and no leaderboards. 

- Goal: Build a fast, lightweight, static marketing website optimized for 50 global languages.
- Source of product truth: `materials/specs/product/Pushanova-Product-Paper.md` (or equivalent product documents).
- Do not invent features. Match existing copy.

## Stack

- Static HTML generated by a simple Node.js build script.
- Vanilla CSS. Use simple, native, modern HTML/CSS paradigms. No hacks, no brittle workarounds, and no magic numbers.
- Localization driven by individual JSON files per language (e.g., `locales/en.json`, `locales/da.json`).
- Hosting: GitHub Pages. The final build output must be 100% static and compatible with standard GitHub Pages deployment.
- NO React, NO Next.js, NO Tailwind, NO complex frameworks.

## Images

- Apple App Store badges stay in `src/images/Download-on-the-App-Store/` with Apple’s filenames. Do not rename or split that folder. Pair Apple’s black (`blk`) badge for light mode with the white (`wht`) badge for dark mode via `<picture>` and `prefers-color-scheme`.
- Product screenshots go in `src/images/screenshots/{locale}/{device}-{subject}.webp`. Hyphens only. Name the picture, not the page (e.g. `iphone-home.webp`, `watch-counting.webp`, `iphone-watch.webp`). Same filename in every language folder. Missing locales fall back to `en`.
- Site mark is `src/images/icon-64.webp` in the header and `src/images/icon-256.webp` in the home H1. Not locale-specific. Favicons stay in `src/static/`.
- Lossless WebP, tight transparent crop. iPhone shots are 800px wide. A composition (Watch + iPhone) is wider; keep the iPhone at 800px so CSS can match sizes. Watch-only shots are about 500px wide and display at the same 16rem column as iPhone shots. One file per language. No `@2x` or `en_US` in names.

## Localization & Translation

- **American English is the Base & Default:** `locales/en.json` is American English (US): the source language and base template. File name, URLs, and `hreflang` stay `en`. HTML `lang` is `en-US`. Switcher label is `English (US)`. Do not add `en-US.json` or rename this locale. British English is `en-GB` (press-ups). All translations originate from this file. Missing screenshots fall back to `en`. A locale file must match this key tree; the build fails on missing strings. It does not fill gaps with English.
- **One Language Per File:** Architecture must be scalable and token-efficient. Do not merge languages into a single giant JSON.
- **Add a language:** One locale per pass. Do not open other `locales/*.json` files. Do not open other `search-phrases/*.md` files. Do not edit templates.
  1. Run the search-phrase pass for that locale only, as in **Search phrases** below. Log the winner in `materials/specs/product/search-phrases/{bcp47}.md`.
  2. Read only `locales/en.json`. Create `locales/{bcp47}.json` with the same tree. Translate `message` values only; omit `description`. Set `meta.code` (HTML `lang`, e.g. `en-US`) and `meta.name` (e.g. `English (US)`).
  3. Write the page `<title>` and meta description from the logged phrase. Do not translate the English title word for word.
  4. Do not translate placeholders, HTML tags, **Pushanova**, Apple product names, or in-app UI labels inside `<strong>`.
  5. Run `npm run build`. The build fails if the key tree, placeholders, or HTML tags do not match English, or if that locale has no App Store badge.
- **Context is Required:** The American English source file (`en.json`) must include descriptive comments for each key. Translators and AI need to know where the string appears, its character limits, and its context. Other locale files may ship `{ "message": "…" }` leaves only.
- **Protect Placeholders:** Never translate variables, placeholders, or HTML tags (e.g., `{rating}`, `{email}`, `<sup>`). They must remain exactly as written in the source.
- **Whole Sentences Only:** Never concatenate text fragments in the code (e.g., never do `"Translated from " + language`). Always translate full strings so grammar structures can shift naturally per language.
- **Write for Translation:** Use simple, direct, and universally understood English. Avoid slang, idioms, abstract terms, or complex sentence structure. 

## Conventions

- **Copy.** Short paragraphs, 1–3 sentences. Say what the feature removes, not how the sensors work. Marketing sections stay paragraphs; support and FAQ stay real questions and answers.
- **Tone.** Welcoming. The product paper wins when a louder search phrase fights the product.
- **Privacy policy is American English only.** Do not localize or translate `materials/copy/privacy.md`.
- **GitHub Pages routing.** Clean URLs are folders with `index.html` (for example `/da/index.html`). Asset paths must work as static files.
- **Dark mode.** `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` only. No JavaScript theme toggle.
- **AEO.** Semantic outline (H1, H2, H3), descriptive `alt`, and one direct sentence an answer engine can quote.
- **Search phrases.** One locale per pass. One file per locale: `materials/specs/product/search-phrases/{bcp47}.md`, same code as `locales/`. Do not start the next language in the same session. Do not open other files in that folder. Spend what that locale needs. Batch calls. Skip backlinks and domain analytics.
  1. Google is the source, via DataForSEO. Seeds: counter, app, tracker, program, challenge. Expand with `keywords_for_keywords` for that location and language. Do not invent a long translation list. Then `POST /v3/keywords_data/google_ads/search_volume/live` with `location_code` and `language_code`, and check the shortlist with `POST /v3/serp/google/organic/live/regular` so a high-volume exercise or challenge query is not treated as an app query.
  2. On the Runet, Yandex Wordstat is the source: Russia and other Russian-speaking markets where Yandex is a real search engine. Use `top_requests` on the phrase. Read queries that contain it. Ignore the similar-phrase list. Wordstat has no bid, so intent comes from those child queries.
  3. If DataForSEO rejects the location or the shortlist has no measurable volume, check the same phrases on Wordstat before giving up. A small Google volume is still data. Do not replace it with a handful of Yandex searches in a Google country. Empty tools do not mean empty demand: a reporting floor is not proof that no one searches. Wordstat is for the Runet; it does not rescue Telugu, Urdu, or other languages Google Ads barely measures. If both sources are empty, still pick the strongest native install phrase you can justify — how people name the exercise, an app, a counter, or a program; native script vs Latin; mixed English. Log that it was unmeasured. Do not fall back to a word-for-word English title.
  4. Pick the strongest phrase a person ready to install would search, and that this site can realistically rank for. On Google, use the bid when two volumes are close. A blank volume does not drop a true product fact such as iPhone or Apple Watch.
  5. Log the winner, why it won, the rejected high-volume phrases, and the tool in `materials/specs/product/search-phrases/{bcp47}.md`, with country, language, and month. If the winner was unmeasured, say so.
  6. Put that phrase in the page `<title>` and meta description. The H1 stays a sentence a person wants to read. Do not add a body sentence just to include a keyword. The homepage can be found for the install phrase and still promise 100 push-ups.

## Styling

- **Mobile-first, full-size on desktop.** Base styles for a phone. At larger viewports, type, spacing, and the reading column grow. Do not keep a phone-width layout on desktop.
- **System sans, Apple first.** `font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;`. No webfonts for marketing copy. Let the OS pick the face for each `lang` (Latin, Indic, CJK, Arabic, and the rest). Exception: the verbatim **Club Pushanova** header wordmark uses self-hosted Inter Tight SemiBold.
- **Tokens** as CSS custom properties. Follow `prefers-color-scheme`.

  Light: page `#FFFFFF`, band `#F5F5F7`, text `#1D1D1F`, accent `#1F6BB5` (steel blue), fill `#0091FF`.
  Dark: page `#000000`, band `#1D1D1F`, text `#F5F5F7`, accent `#7EABD8` (same hue, lightened and softened). Fill is `#0091FF` mixed toward black so white label text still holds.

  Use accent for links and emphasis only. Do not use the dark-mode accent as a solid fill. Do not put white text on raw `#0091FF` in dark mode; darken the fill instead.
- **Logical properties** for spacing and alignment. RTL is a first-class layout.
- **Line-height** stays open enough for Indic and other complex scripts (~1.4–1.6). Fluid type with `clamp()` so desktop is not 14px body text. Main column max-width in `ch` or `rem`, not a fixed pixel page.
- **Touch-first:** no hover-only actions; visible focus; wrap, don’t truncate, translated UI. `overflow-wrap` for long words and URLs.
- **Do not** use letter-spacing on copy, `text-transform: uppercase` on translated UI, or `text-align: justify`.

## Do not

- Do not add accounts, ads, leaderboards, streaks-as-punishment, or social features to the site story.
- Do not invent new features or hardware requirements. 
- Do not generate complex CSS grids, "Bento Boxes," or heavy UI mockups unless explicitly instructed. Stick to plain, semantic HTML tags.
- Do not hardcode American English marketing text into the final HTML files (all text routes through the isolated language JSON files).